Let $\vec{g} \sim \Normdistr{\vec{0}}{\I{n}}$ be a standard Gaussian random vector. 
The \emph{Gaussian width} of a bounded set $K\subset \R^n$ is defined as 
\begin{equation}
\meanwidth{K}\coloneqq\mathbb{E}[\sup_{\vec{x} \in K}\sp{\vec{g}}{\vec{x}}].
\end{equation}
\end{definition}
See Figure~\ref{} for an illustration of the Gaussian width and its geometric meaning.
Since our task is to estimate a fixed vector $\vec{x}_0$, it is natural to measure the complexity of the signal set $K$ in a small neighborhood of $\vec{x}_0$, rather than computing the ``global'' width $\meanwidth{K}$.
To this end, let us consider the following localized version: For $t>0$, the \emph{local Gaussian width} of a subset $K\subset \R^n$ at scale $t>0$ is given by
\begin{equation}
 \meanwidth[t]{K}\coloneqq\meanwidth{K\intersec t\ball[2][n]}.
\end{equation}
In particular, we call $\meanwidth[t]{K-\vec{x}_0}$ the \emph{local Gaussian width} of $K$ in $\vec{x}_0$ at scale $t>0$; see also Figure~\ref{}.
Note that the local Gaussian width is always bounded from above by its global counterpart:
\begin{equation}\label{}
 \meanwidth[t]{K}=\mathbb{E}[\sup_{\vec{x} \in K\intersec t\ball[2][n]}\sp{\vec{g}}{\vec{x}}] \leq \mathbb{E}[\sup_{\vec{x} \in K}\sp{\vec{g}}{\vec{x}}]=\meanwidth{K}.
\end{equation}

