\paragraph{History and previous results.}

Sumsets $B + C \coloneqq \{ b + c : b \in B, c \in C \}$ for $B,C \subset \mathbb{N}$ are a central object of study in additive combinatorics.
In particular, it is natural to ask which sets $A\subset\mathbb{N}$ contain a sumset $B + C$ with $B$ and $C$ infinite.
It follows from the infinite version of  Ramsey's theorem \cite[Theorem~A]{MR1576401} that, whenever $\mathbb{N}$ is finitely partitioned, one of the cells contains $B+C$ for infinite sets $B,C \subset \mathbb{N}$; this is also an immediate corollary of Hindman's theorem~\cite{Hindman79}.
