\subsection{McMullen's absolute game}\label{}

McMullen's \textit{absolute game} \cite{McMullen_absolute_winning}
is defined in such a way that instead of choosing a region where Bob
must play, Alice now chooses a region where Bob must not play. To be
precise, given $0<\beta<1$, the $\beta$ absolute game is played as
follows: Bob first picks some initial ball $B_0 \subseteq X$. Alice and
Bob then take turns to place successive closed balls in such a way
that

\begin{equation}\label{}
B_0 \: \supseteq\: B_0 \setminus A_1 \:\supseteq \:B_1 \supseteq \cdots \supseteq\: B_i \: \supseteq \: B_i \setminus A_{i+1} \:\supseteq \:B_{i+1} \:\supseteq \: \cdots,
\end{equation}
subject to the conditions

\begin{equation}\label{}
\mathrm{rad}(A_{i+1}) \leq \beta \cdot \mathrm{rad}(B_i) \quad \textrm{  and  } \quad \mathrm{rad}(B_{i+1}) \geq \beta \cdot \mathrm{rad}(B_i) \quad \textrm{  for all  } i \geq 0.
\end{equation}
