gnal estimation methods can be formulated as an optimization problem of the form
\begin{equation}\label{}
 \min_{\vec{x} \in \R^n} \tfrac{1}{m} \sum_{i = 1}^m \mathcal{L}(\sp{\a_i}{\vec{x}},y_i) \quad \text{subject to \quad $\vec{x} \in K$,}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{L}\colon \R \times \R \to \R$ is a convex \emph{loss function} and $K \subset \R^n$ defines a convex constraint set, usually called the \emph{signal set}.
The purpose of $\mathcal{L}$ is to assess how well the candidate model $\a_i \mapsto \sp{\a_i}{\vec{x}}$ matches with the true outputs $y_i$.
With regard to our initial recovery task, we may therefore hope that a minimizer $\vec{\hat{x}} \in \R^n$ of \eqref{} provides an accurate approximation of the signal vector $\vec{x}_0$. On the other hand, the signal set $K$ encodes certain structural hypotheses about $\vec{x}_0$, e.g., sparsity.
A prototypical choice is an $\l{1}$-constraint, i.e., $K = R \ball[1][n]$ for some $R > 0$, which indeed often serves as a powerful sparsity prior if $m \ll n$.

In this work, we will focus on a special instance of \eqref{} that is based on the so-called \emph{hinge loss} given by $\mathcal{L}^{\text{hng}}(v) \coloneqq \pospart{1 - v} \coloneqq \max\{0, 1 - v\}$ for $v \in \R$.
Using $\mathcal{L}(v,v') \coloneqq \mathcal{L}^{\text{hng}}(v \cdot v')$ as loss function, the program of \eqref{} now reads as follows:
\begin{equation}\label{}
 \min_{\vec{x} \in \R^n} \tfrac{1}{m} \sum_{i = 1}^m \max\{0, 1 - y_i \sp{\a_i}{\vec{x}}\} \quad \text{subject to \quad $\vec{x} \in K$.}
\end{equation}
This estimator is specifically tailored to deal with binary observations:
Intuitively, by minimizing the objective functional of \eqref{}, one tries to select $\vec{x} \in K$ in such a way that the sign of $\sp{\a_i}{\vec{x}}$ equals $y_i \in \{-1,+1\}$ for most of the samples $i \in \{1, \dots, m\}$.
With other words, a solution $\vec{\hat{x}}$ to \eqref{} yields a predictor $\a_i \mapsto \sign(\sp{\a_i}{\vec{\hat{x}}})$ of the true outputs $y_i$.
