\paragraph{Learnt denoising process}

Recall $q(\x_{t-1}|\x_t) = \frac{q(\x_t|\x_{t-1})q(\x_{t-1})}{q(\x_t)}$. The posterior (i.e. true denoising process) $q(\x_{t-1}|\x_t)$ is approximately Gaussian, a key insight. This is because $q(\x_t|\x_{t-1})$ is a normal distribution with variance $\beta_t \ll 1$ and $q(\x_{t-1})$ does not vary a lot over the density of $q(\x_t|\x_{t-1})$. As such, the product of their probability density functions will be bell-shaped. Additionally, $q(\x_t)$ is a normalisation factor. See \autoref{fig:theory:bishop} for an illustrative example.

